<!-- NEW CONCEPT: ENUMERATE -->
<section class="oop-section">
    <div class="oop-section-header">
        <div class="oop-section-icon">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i>
        </div>
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">New Concept: enumerate()</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="concept-explanation" style="background: #f8f9fa; border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">When looping through a list, sometimes you need both the <strong>item</strong> AND its <strong>position</strong>. That's what <code>enumerate()</code> does.</p>
        
        <div style="display: grid; gap: 1rem; margin: 1rem 0;">
            <div style="background: #1a1a2e; color: #f8f9fa; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; font-family: 'Fira Code', monospace; font-size: 0.9rem;">
                <div style="color: #6c757d; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"># Printing a shopping list with numbers</div>
                <div>shopping = ["milk", "bread", "eggs"]</div>
                <div><span style="color: #c9a227;">for</span> i, item <span style="color: #c9a227;">in</span> <span style="color: #c9a227;">enumerate</span>(shopping):</div>
                <div style="padding-left: 1.5rem;">print(i, item)</div>
                <div style="color: #6c757d; margin-top: 0.5rem;"># Output:</div>
                <div style="color: #6c757d;"># 0 milk</div>
                <div style="color: #6c757d;"># 1 bread</div>
                <div style="color: #6c757d;"># 2 eggs</div>
            </div>
        </div>
        
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>Key detail:</strong> The index starts at <code>0</code>, not <code>1</code>. If you want to display numbers starting from 1, you'll need to adjust.</p>
        
        <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Think about:</strong> How could you use this to display numbered choices to a user?</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- FUNCTION SPECIFICATION -->
<section class="oop-section">
    <div class="oop-section-header">
        <div class="oop-section-icon">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-code"></i>
        </div>
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Function Specification</h2>
    </div>
    
    <p class="section-intro">Add this method to your Player class in <code>student/player.py</code>.</p>
    
    <details class="skeleton-block" open>
        <summary class="skeleton-summary">
            <span class="skeleton-step">1</span>
            <span class="skeleton-title">respond_to_npc(options)</span>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down skeleton-chevron"></i>
        </summary>
        <div class="skeleton-content">
            <div class="challenge-spec" style="border: 1px solid #dee2e6; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;">
                <div style="background: var(--primary-color); color: white; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; font-weight: 600;">
                    Display choices and get user selection
                </div>
                <div style="padding: 1rem;">
                    <p><strong>Parameter:</strong> <code>options</code> — a list of dialogue choices (strings)</p>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Returns:</strong> The index (0-based) of the chosen option</p>
                    
                    <div style="background: #f8f9fa; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 1rem;">
                        <strong>What it should do:</strong>
                        <ol style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                            <li>Display each option with a number (1, 2, 3...)</li>
                            <li>Ask the user to enter their choice</li>
                            <li>Validate the input (must be a number in range)</li>
                            <li>Keep asking until valid input is given</li>
                            <li>Return the 0-based index</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    
                    <div style="background: #f8f9fa; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 1rem;">
                        <strong>Example:</strong>
                        <pre style="margin: 0.5rem 0; font-size: 0.85rem;">options = ["Ask about the station", "Request supplies", "Leave"]

# Your function should display:
#   1. Ask about the station
#   2. Request supplies
#   3. Leave
# Then prompt: "Enter choice: "

# If user types "2", return 1 (0-based index)
# If user types "abc" or "99", ask again</pre>
                    </div>
                    
                    <div style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 1rem; border-left: 4px solid #ffc107;">
                        <strong>💡 Key insight:</strong> Users see 1, 2, 3 but Python lists start at 0. You'll need to convert between these.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </details>
</section>

